// Command-line arguments — the file six applets in this directory imported and nobody had written.
//
// The shipped `packages/box/src/lib/args.wac` is 458 lines and a struct of twelve fields, and its own
// doc comments are the best argument against its shape: **five of the twelve exist only because
// parsing cannot fail.** `parseArgs` returns an `Args` come what may, so every way a command line can
// be wrong is a field the applet has to remember to look at.
//
//     string badNum;        // "The value given for a numeric option that is not a number"
//     string missingValue;  // "The letter of a flag that takes a value and was given none"
//     string longOpt;       // "The first `--long` option given" — kept so a refusal can name it
//     bool   hasNum;        // absence, because 0 was both "absent" and "the user asked for zero"
//     string numSign;       // `""`, `"+"` or `"-"` — three states in a string
//
// Each of the first three is documented with the bug it was added for, and all three read the same:
//
// > It used to be nothing at all: the parser ran out of arguments, recorded the flag without a value,
// > and the applet fell back to its default, so `head -n` printed ten lines and exited 0 where GNU
// > says "option requires an argument -- 'n'" and exits 1. **A mistake in the command came back as an
// > answer, which is the worst of the three things it could do.**
//
// That sentence appears twice in the shipped file, for two different fields. It is the same bug
// twice and the fix was the same both times — add a field and check it. Measuring whether the fix
// holds is what this file is for, and the first measurement was wrong in a way worth keeping:
//
//     applets reading `a.num`:                     16
//     ...that ever mention `a.badNum`:              6      -> "ten of sixteen never check it"
//
// That counts applets whose `badNum` can never be set. It is only written by a **valued** flag —
// `takesValue(name, letter)` decides — and eight of the ten have no valued flag at all, so not
// reading it is correct. The honest denominator is eight, and the honest answer is **six of eight**:
// `base64` and `base32` are the two, and `base64 -w x` really does wrap at the default 76 and exit 0
// where `head -n x` refuses and exits 1. Both run rather than read.
//
// Narrowing it produced the better finding, because the five failure-carrying fields split cleanly:
//
// | field | who reads it | how often it is right |
// |---|---|---|
// | `missingValue`, `longOpt` | `lib/flags.wac`'s `refuseFlags`, called first by **every** applet | always |
// | `badNum` | each applet, by hand | 6 of 8 |
//
// One parser, one file set, one set of conventions, and the only variable is **whether something
// shared does the checking**. `base64 -w` with no value refuses correctly in an applet that never
// mentions the field; `base64 -w x` does not, in the same applet, for the field it must read itself.
//
// > **A function that cannot fail moves its failures into its output, and an output field is a
// > request rather than a requirement — unless something shared reads it.**
//
// `issues/system/0340a` carries the two applets and the four that still spell their default
// `a.num == 0 ? d : a.num`, which `hasNum`'s own comment says was removed from every applet.
//
// So this is not a tidier parser. It is the same parser with the failures moved from twelve fields
// into a return type, which is the third answer: not *remember to check*, not *hope a shared
// function checks for you*, but *the compiler will not let you not check*.

import { Result, Vec, Map } from "core";

/** Every way a command line can be wrong, which the shipped parser spells as five empty strings. */
export struct UnknownFlag  { string spelled; }
export struct MissingValue { string flag; }
export struct NotANumber   { string flag; string given; }
export struct LongOption   { string spelled; }
export union<UnknownFlag, MissingValue, NotANumber, LongOption> ArgFault;

/**
 * Which of GNU's three forms a count was written in — `10`, `+10`, `-10`.
 *
 * `numSign` was a `string` holding `""`, `"+"` or `"-"`, and only `head` and `tail` read it, in
 * opposite directions. Three states in a type with billions is the shape this directory has counted
 * eight times; here it costs nothing to fix because there are exactly three and they are named.
 */
export enum Sign { Plain, Forward, Backward }

/** A numeric option that was given. Absent is `Count?`, which is what `hasNum` was. */
export struct Count { i32 magnitude; Sign sign; }

/**
 * What an applet accepts, declared by the applet.
 *
 * The shipped parser grew this as `takesValue(string name, i32 letter)` — a function keyed on the
 * *applet's name*, with a table of every applet in the box inside the shared library. That is the
 * dependency running the wrong way: `cut` and `fold` are named in the parser, so adding an applet
 * means editing a file it does not own, and the parser cannot be tested without knowing what a `cut`
 * is. Here the applet hands its spec in, and the table goes away.
 */
export struct Spec {
  /** Letters that are flags on their own: `-i`, `-v`. */
  string flags;
  /** Letters that take a value: `-d,` and `-d ,`. */
  string valued;
  /** Whether a bare `-5`, `+5` or `-n 5` is a count for this applet. */
  bool counts;
}

/**
 * A parsed command line, and there is nothing in it that is not an answer.
 *
 * Seven fields became four, and the three that went were not merged into the others — they were
 * failures, and failures are in the return type now.
 */
export struct Args {
  /** What to call this program in its own messages. */
  string name;
  /** Positional arguments, in order. */
  string[] operands;
  /** The letters of every flag given, in no particular order. */
  string flags;
  /** The count, if one was given and this applet takes one. */
  Count? count;
  /** The value of each valued flag, by letter. */
  Map<string, string> values;
}

/**
 * Parse `argv` against `spec`.
 *
 * `argv` is the applet's own arguments and does not contain its name — which is the whole of what the
 * shipped `argv`/`own`/`rest` triple was about. That file kept three views of one array because
 * *"where an applet's arguments start inside it depends on how it was launched"*, and the answer is
 * that the launcher knows and the applet does not need to: `box echo -n hi` and `bin/echo -n hi`
 * differ in the *caller*, so the caller slices.
 */
export Result<Args, ArgFault> parse(string name, string[] argv, const Spec spec) { … }

/** Whether flag `letter` was given. */
export bool has(const Args a, string letter) { … }

/** `cp: unrecognized option -- 'q'`, matching GNU because the shipped one already does. */
export string say(string name, ArgFault f) { … }

// ## 1. What the return type is worth, counted rather than asserted
//
// The `badNum` guard is copy-pasted: six applets contain the same line to the byte, and two do not
// have it. With a `Result` the six copies and the two absences are both gone, replaced by one
// `match` per applet — not a `try`, because an applet has to print `say(name, f)` and exit 1 rather
// than propagate.
//
// So the saving is not the character count, and it is not even *checking versus not checking*, since
// `refuseFlags` already shows that a shared reader gets it right every time. It is that **a shared
// reader has to exist before the failure does.** That is not a guess about anyone's attention; the
// three fields were all added on 2026-08-07 and the log gives the hour:
//
//     10:45  d92a7a94c  sh, box: a count that is not a count, and a sign that means something
//                       -> `badNum`
//     15:41  650e656f3  box: refuse the flag rather than ignoring it, in one place
//                       -> `refuseFlags`
//     15:59  25b8eb108  box: a long option is one thing, not a run of short ones
//                       -> `longOpt`, read by `refuseFlags`
//     19:32  03f1da9bc  box: an operand error says what the real tool says
//                       -> `missingValue`, read by `refuseFlags`
//
// **`badNum` predates the shared reader by five hours.** Both failures invented after `refuseFlags`
// existed went into it; the one invented before it never moved. So the split in the table above is
// not about which failure is subtle or which author was careful — it is about which side of 15:41 a
// field was born on, and 15:41's own commit message is *"in one place"*.
//
// Three data points is three data points. But the mechanism it names does not need many, because it
// is not a tendency: a shared reader can only read fields that exist when someone writes it, and
// nothing goes back to add the older ones. **A convention adopted midway through covers the second
// half of a file and looks, afterwards, exactly like a convention that is followed.**
//
// ## 2. And the reason `badNum` was unread is not carelessness
//
// It is worth being precise, because *the two applets should have checked* is not a finding. The
// parser **succeeded**. `parseArgs(cli)` answers an `Args` and there is nothing in the call to
// suggest a question was left open — the value looks complete, the applet's own logic type-checks,
// and it runs. `base64.wac` is code a careful author could write with the parser's interface open in
// front of them and never see the obligation, and the proof is that the same author's `refuseFlags`
// call two lines above handles two other failures perfectly, invisibly, by being a call.
//
// `@/packages/box/src/cp.wac` found the mirror of this yesterday: `FileFault` has 29 dead arms and
// nobody pays for them, because every consumer in the directory `try`s or discards. Here the union
// does not exist and the cost is real and shipped. Two halves of one measurement — **an error type
// with no exhaustive consumer costs nothing, and an error field with no compelled reader costs
// everything** — and the difference between them is entirely whether the compiler is involved.
//
// And the correction is the third data point, about the measuring rather than the design: the first
// count here was 10 of 16 and it was wrong because it never asked *which of these can even reach the
// field*. That is the same mistake as the 29 dead arms, which counted (operation, member) pairs
// without asking which pairs a caller ever holds at once. **Twice in two days I have counted a
// cross-product and called it a defect rate**, and both times the fix was to ask what actually
// reaches the thing being counted. It did not make either finding go away; it made both sharper.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **`spec` is a value and `has(a, "n")` is a string, so nothing checks one against the other.**
// The `Spec` fixes the dependency direction and does not make the flag letters checkable: an applet
// can declare `flags: "rn"` and then ask `has(a, "f")`, which answers `false` forever. I wrote
// exactly that class of call one commit ago — `@/packages/box/src/cp.wac`'s `a.has("n")` — and it is
// right only because I also wrote the spec, in my head, and never wrote it down. The ask is a way for
// a value to constrain a later literal, which is dependent typing at the small end and is not
// anywhere on `../../../../GRAMMAR.md`. A `Spec` of *named* fields (`struct CpFlags { bool n; bool r; }`)
// would be checked and cannot be parsed generically, which is the trade the shipped `string flags`
// made without stating it.
//
// **`Map<string, string> values` should be `Map<u8, string>` and `u8` is not a key.** Flags are
// single bytes; the shipped file packs the pair into a `string[]` where *"the first byte is the
// flag"*, which is the packed-key shape `../../../../QUESTIONS.md` already has from `@/packages/box`'s
// own `lines`. A one-byte key spelled as a `string` allocates per lookup and admits `"ab"`, which is
// not a flag.
//
// **`ArgFault` carries no position.** `cp -q a b` should say which argument was wrong, and
// `UnknownFlag { string spelled; }` says which *letter*. An index into `argv` is the obvious field
// and it is the shape `@/packages/git/example/ignorelint.wac` filed as *an index into a `Vec` the
// finding does not hold* — third instance, and here the container is the caller's own parameter, so
// the fault could carry it and would then keep the array alive.
